From a6252be8fd8b10bdbba241c7ebbc36b2bcfcb044 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:13:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/11] feat(#95): typing.Protocols for the duck-typed results
 the fault-flag readers receive

The plan item #95 names and nobody built: "with typing.Protocols for the duck-typed results so ty checks
every read". The renderers take their results unannotated (`def _final_banner_status(result)`), so a
type checker could not see a direct fault-flag read and a rename was caught only at runtime.

Added, beside the types they describe: `converge.ConvergeFaults`, `audit.SuiteAuditFaults` and
`equivalence.SurvivorReportFaults`, each a Protocol of read-only properties naming exactly the fault
flags readers take off that type. The cli readers converted in this series now annotate their `result` /
`a` / `rep` parameter with them (TYPE_CHECKING imports, so nothing is loaded at runtime): _completeness_verdict,
_plain_terms, _final_banner_status, _floor_caused, _converge_blocked, _contradicted_flag_lines,
_verdict_flag_pairs, _missing_line_report, _audit_measurement_block, _audit_code, _redundant_kept,
_audit_closing_action. A new test asserts every name a Protocol lists is a field of the real type, so a
Protocol cannot drift into a fiction.

Not closed: no type-checker gate was added to CI (out of scope by instruction), and the larger
orchestrators (_format_converge, _converge_decision, _converge_action, _format_audit) are not yet
annotated; their reads are direct and guarded at runtime and by the absorption guard.

The four guard files and tests/test_fault_flags_direct_intent.py pass under 3.14 and 3.12 except the
`bound` entry noted in the previous commit; ruff clean under pinned 0.16.7.
